Single-database configuration for Flask.

End User authentication: online PostgreSQL rollout
==================================================

Use one migration runner and retain the PR's revision IDs if any environment
has stamped them. The PostgreSQL migrations commit nullable-column changes
before data scans. An independently committed index build or drop can finish
before Alembic records its revision. Rerun the upgrade/downgrade to reconcile a
matching healthy index or an already completed drop. Inspect conflicting indexes
and abandoned invalid builds before retrying; never drop a healthy index as
routine recovery. Offline SQL assumes the declared starting schema and must
be executed with its transaction boundaries intact.

Builder origin (revision b43e8c1d9a72) is backfilled in 1,000-account batches.
In an online rollout the first pass can run with the legacy release serving.
A legacy write can land behind a batch cursor or after the first pass finishes.
Before enabling the new Account/Workspace writers:

1. Complete the schema upgrade and initial backfill with the new image.
2. Drain legacy writes that create accounts or change account initialization or
   workspace ownership, including in-flight requests and jobs. Keep new writers
   that depend on the origin column stopped until the next step succeeds;
   otherwise NULL may be either legacy data or a legitimate new value.
3. Run the cutover pass once using the new image and the migration database role:

       flask finalize-builder-origin-backfill --confirm-before-cutover

4. Start the new writers only after the cutover pass succeeds.

Retry step 3 with writers still drained if interrupted. Committed origins are
not overwritten, and accounts with no legacy origin remain NULL. Do not run the
legacy pass after the new onboarding flow has started writing. For a stopped
upgrade with no overlapping legacy writes, the initial pass is sufficient.
MySQL retains its existing DDL and portable backfill path.

